    "title": "How Does Pomona College Rank Among America's Best Colleges?",
    "text": "Pomona College is a private institution that was founded in 1887. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Pomona College is ranked No. #7 in National Liberal Arts Colleges. It's also ranked No. #5 in Best Value Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 1,766 (fall 2024), its setting is suburban, and the campus size is 140 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Pomona College is 7:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Pomona College accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's tuition and fees are $68,670. Fifty-five percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $18,084. The four-year graduation rate is 61%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $60,850.Pomona College is a private institution that was founded in 1887. In the 2026 edition of Best Colleges, Pomona College is ranked No. #7 in National Liberal Arts Colleges. It's also ranked No. #5 in Best Value Schools. It has a total undergraduate enrollment of 1,766 (fall 2024), its setting is suburban, and the campus size is 140 acres. The student-faculty ratio at Pomona College is 7:1, and it utilizes a semester-based academic calendar. Pomona College accepts the Common Application and has a test-optional admissions policy. The school's tuition and fees are $68,670. Fifty-five percent of first-year students receive need-based financial aid, and the average net price for federal loan recipients is $18,084. The four-year graduation rate is 61%. Six years after graduation, the median salary for graduates is $60,850.",
